DESCRIPTION:Europe is commonly narrated from its historical centers. Polit
 ical ideas\, cultural innovation\, and modernity itself are assumed to ori
 ginate in places such as Paris\, London\, or Berlin before spreading to th
 e continent's margins. Georgia usually appears in this narrative\, if at a
 ll\, as a distant periphery whose history merely reflects developments els
 ewhere.\n\nThis lecture argues that such a view is historically misleading
 . Rather than treating Georgia as Europe's edge\, it proposes to read it a
 s o\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-10-02 11:14 PDT
LOCATION:Encina Commons
END:VEVENT
